<Base description="Set up the FanSnipe Docker studio, unlock your browser, preview a stream and save a recording on your own device." title="FanSnipe Studio Setup and Recording Guide">
<div class="wrap"><article class="prose guide">
<h1>Open your studio.</h1>
<p class="lead">The computer running FanSnipe is where your recordings live. Docker serves a complete studio on that computer. Once you unlock your browser, you can preview, record, and play everything from the same tab.</p>
<p><a href={local}>Open local studio</a> · <a href="/downloads/">Get the companion</a> · <a href="/app/">Use hosted studio</a></p>

<h2 id="docker-setup">Set up your studio password</h2>
<p>With Docker companion 0.1.4 or later, choose a password of at least eight characters. Save it on the Docker host in a plain-text file named <code>fansnipe_studio_password</code>, with no filename extension. Keep the file private. If the local studio offers <strong>Save setup password file</strong>, you can make this file right in the browser.</p>
<div class="setup-cards">
<section><h3>Docker Desktop</h3>
<p>When configuring the container, open <strong>Optional settings → Volumes</strong>. Add the password file as a read-only mount, alongside your existing data and media volumes.</p>
<figure aria-label="Password file volume mapping"><span>Host path</span><code>…/fansnipe_studio_password</code><span>Container path</span><code>/run/secrets/fansnipe_studio_password</code></figure>
<p>Start the container, then choose <strong>Open in browser</strong>. If a setup folder is already mounted at <code>/run/secrets</code>, save the file inside that folder and choose <strong>Check again</strong> in the studio.</p>
</section>
<section><h3>Docker Compose</h3>
<p>Save the password file next to the existing FanSnipe Compose file. Include the supplied <a href="https://github.com/Fanclan/FanSnipe-Companion/blob/main/compose.studio-password.yaml">password overlay</a> when starting the project. Keep the same project name and the existing recording and catalog volumes.</p>
<figure aria-label="Compose project files"><span>In your FanSnipe project</span><code>compose.yaml</code><code>compose.studio-password.yaml</code><code>fansnipe_studio_password</code></figure>
<p><code>docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.studio-password.yaml up -d</code></p>
</section>
</div>
<h2>Unlock your browser</h2>
<p>On the Docker host, open <a href={local}>127.0.0.1:47821/app/</a> and enter your password. A remembered session takes you straight back to your recordings. To reset the password, replace the mounted password file and recreate the container with the same volumes. If Docker runs on a NAS, open that host’s explicitly configured HTTPS studio.</p>
<p>Existing hosted sessions continue to work. The local studio has its own browser session and asks for your password the first time. The macOS and Windows desktop companions continue to show an approval dialog for new browsers.</p>
<h2>Preview and record</h2>
<ol><li>Paste a supported stream URL into Studio Home. The companion looks up the broadcast title, channel, and availability. Choose <strong>Edit title</strong> for a different name.</li>
<li>Choose <strong>Play preview</strong> to watch, or <strong>Record</strong> to start saving immediately. Quality and format are under recording options. Playback begins muted.</li>
<li>Watch the capture card for elapsed time and bytes saved. Closing the player or browser keeps the recording running. Select <strong>Stop recording</strong> when you are done.</li>
<li>After finishing, play the recording from the card or find it in your library.</li></ol>
<h3>Supported stream links</h3>
<p>Use a live Twitch, Kick, or Picarto channel, a YouTube Live video, or a public room on Chaturbate, Stripchat, or BongaCams. Record streams that the broadcaster and platform permit you to save. Save an offline channel to check it again or enable automatic recording later.</p>
<h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
<details><summary>My local studio will not open</summary><p>Check that the companion or Docker container is running on this computer and that port 47821 is published. Docker Desktop’s port link opens that instance. On another device, localhost points to that device; use your configured studio address instead.</p></details>
<details><summary>The password screen says setup is needed</summary><p>Check the exact filename and container mount shown above. Some editors add <code>.txt</code> automatically. If you changed a single-file mount, recreate the container to pick up the replacement. A studio without a configured password cannot be claimed by an arbitrary visitor.</p></details>
<details><summary>I'm using an older companion or the hosted bridge</summary><p>Existing pairings and libraries continue working. The hosted Docker bridge is optional and uses <code>FANSNIPE_HOSTED_DASHBOARD=true</code> with a loopback port. For older Docker releases, use the local operator approval and setup token described in that release. Upgrade via <a href="/downloads/">Downloads</a> for password unlock and moving previews.</p></details>
<details><summary>My preview is unavailable, or a recording shows 0 B</summary><p>Preview playback depends on browser codec support. A usable stream can still be recorded when the preview player cannot play it. Open a failed recording for its reason. A zero-byte entry has no media to recover. Check that the stream is publicly playable and start a fresh recording after correcting the problem.</p></details>
</article></div></Base>
